Playwright tests in this repo follow user-facing locators, web-first waiting, composed POMs, setup-project auth, self-cleaning data, and hook-enforced refusals — details below.

**1. Locators** — Prefer roles and accessible names; reserve test ids for last resort; CSS/XPath needs review.  
**2. Waiting** — Assert on locators with auto-retry; never sleep or snapshot visibility booleans.  
**3. Page Object Model** — One class per page/component; constructor holds locators; methods act; specs assert.  
**4. Auth** — Setup project + `storageState`; second family via `newContext`; env credentials only.  
**5. Test data** — Unique, isolated, cleaned up; no shared-env coupling between students.  
**6. Tags** — One tag on each `test()`; `@destructive` only for shared global state + `--workers=1`.  
**7. Test data sources** — Factories, static invalid sets, env secrets, route/label enums.  
**8. API** — `request` for seed/teardown/contracts; not UI for prerequisites.  
**9. Network mocking** — Stub dependencies and failure modes; never the endpoint under test.  
**10. Accessibility** — axe WCAG scans + keyboard paths; roles prove names.  
**11. Assertions** — Web-first; soft for bundles; screenshots with masks only.  
**12. Anti-flake** — CI retries, isolation, clocks, locale/timezone, trace — not brute workers/retries.

## 1. Locators

Use the locator priority chain: `getByRole` → `getByLabel` / `getByPlaceholder` → `getByText` → `getByTestId`. CSS and XPath are smells — flag for review, do not default to them.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save appointment' }).click();
await page.getByLabel('Child name').fill(childName);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.locator('#save-btn').click();
await page.locator('//button[contains(@class,"primary")]').click();
```

**Why:** Role and label locators match how users and assistive tech find controls; CSS/XPath break on markup churn.

## 2. Waiting

Use web-first `expect(locator)` (and locator actions that auto-wait). Never `waitForTimeout`. Never `expect(await locator.isVisible())` or other boolean snapshots.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
await page.getByRole('link', { name: 'Schedule' }).click();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
await expect(await page.getByText('Dashboard').isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** Playwright retries assertions and actions against real UI state; sleeps and boolean snapshots race the app.

## 3. Page Object Model

One POM per page or major component. Declare locators in the constructor. Methods perform user actions only — no `expect()` inside POMs (assert in specs or dedicated helpers).

✅ **Good**

```typescript
export class SchedulePage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {
    this.addSlotButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add slot' });
  }
  private readonly addSlotButton: Locator;

  async openAddSlot(): Promise<void> {
    await this.addSlotButton.click();
  }
}
// spec: await expect(schedule.addSlotButton).toBeEnabled();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
export class SchedulePage extends BasePage {
  async addSlotAndAssert(): Promise<void> {
    await this.page.locator('.add').click();
    expect(await this.page.locator('.toast').isVisible()).toBeTruthy();
  }
}
```

**Why:** POMs stay reusable action surfaces; assertions stay in tests where failures are readable.

## 4. Auth

Log in once per family in the **setup** project and persist `storageState` (`AUTH_FILE` main, `ALT_AUTH_FILE` second parent). Two-family flows: default context uses main state; open `browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE })` for the alt user. Credentials from `process.env` only — never log in inside every test.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
// auth.setup.ts — setup project
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.APP_USER_EMAIL!);
await page.context().storageState({ path: AUTH_FILE });

// spec — app project already has AUTH_FILE
const alt = await browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE });
const altPage = await alt.newPage();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('parent@example.com');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('Secret123!');
});
```

**Why:** One login per family keeps suites fast and matches `playwright.config` project dependencies.

## 5. Test data

Every test creates **unique** data (factory or `Date.now()` suffix). Clean up in `afterEach` / API teardown. Tests must not depend on order or on another student's rows in the shared environment.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
const slotTitle = `Slot-${Date.now()}`;
await schedule.createSlot(slotTitle);
await test.afterEach(async ({ request }) => {
  await deleteSlotViaApi(request, slotTitle);
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
const slotTitle = 'Monday-3pm'; // same title for every run
// no teardown — leaves rows for the next student
```

**Why:** Shared BuddyTime-style envs collide without unique data and explicit cleanup.

## 6. Tags

Exactly **one** tag per `test()`: `@smoke` (every PR), `@sanity` (every push), `@regression` (full suite, manual dispatch), `@api`, `@e2e`, `@destructive` (ONLY shared/global state both families depend on — run with `--workers=1`). Never tag `describe()`. Own-data tests are not `@destructive`. Any mutating test reverts state in `afterEach` / `afterAll`.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('creates a personal note @e2e', async ({ page }) => { /* ... */ });
test('changes org-wide holiday calendar @destructive', async ({ page }) => { /* revert in afterAll */ });
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.describe('calendar @smoke', () => {
  test('adds slot', async ({ page }) => {});
});
test('updates my profile theme @destructive', async ({ page }) => {}); // own data only
```

**Why:** Tags drive CI filters; `@destructive` serializes only true shared-state risk.

## 7. Test data sources

Factories under `test-data/factories/` (Faker + `Date.now()`), static invalid sets in `test-data/*.ts`, secrets and base URLs from `process.env`, and repeated strings in enums such as `test-data/routes.ts`.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import { buildInvitePayload } from '../test-data/factories/invite.factory';
import { AppRoute } from '../test-data/routes';

await page.goto(`${process.env.APP_URL}${AppRoute.TeamSettings}`);
const payload = buildInvitePayload(); // unique email each run
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.goto('https://buddytime.example/team');
await page.getByLabel('Invite email').fill('teammate@test.com');
const path = '/settings/team'; // duplicated in five specs
```

**Why:** Central factories, enums, and env vars prevent drift and leaked secrets in specs.

## 8. API

Use the **`request`** fixture for setup, teardown, and contract checks. Do not create prerequisite data through the UI when a backing API exists.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {
  await seedAppointment(request, { title: uniqueTitle });
});
test('shows appointment in UI @e2e', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/schedule');
  await expect(page.getByText(uniqueTitle)).toBeVisible();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('shows appointment @e2e', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/schedule');
  // 15 UI steps to create the same row the API could seed in one call
});
```

**Why:** API setup is faster, flakier UI paths are avoided, and tests focus on the UI under verification.

## 9. Network mocking

Use `page.route` / `route.fulfill` to simulate **dependencies** — 500, 503, timeout, empty list, malformed JSON. Never mock the endpoint whose behavior the test is meant to prove.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/notifications/**', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: 'Service Unavailable' }),
);
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toContainText('Notifications unavailable');
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/appointments/**', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ json: { id: 'fake', ok: true } }),
); // endpoint under test
```

**Why:** Mocking the SUT hides real integration bugs; mock upstream failures instead.

## 10. Accessibility

Run `@axe-core/playwright` with `wcag2a` / `wcag2aa` on key pages. Add a keyboard-operability check (Tab / Enter). Working `getByRole(..., { name })` locators demonstrate accessible names exist.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
  .analyze();
expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
await page.keyboard.press('Tab');
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Continue' })).toBeFocused();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.locator('div.sidebar >> text=Continue').click(); // no name, no scan
```

**Why:** Automated WCAG checks plus keyboard paths catch regressions roles alone might miss.

## 11. Assertions

Default to web-first `expect(locator)`. Use `expect.soft` when reporting multiple independent checks. `toHaveScreenshot` only with dynamic regions masked — never full-page shots of live clocks or avatars unmasked.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect.soft(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Settings' })).toBeVisible();
await expect.soft(page.getByText(uniqueTitle)).toBeVisible();
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot({ mask: [page.locator('.avatar')] });
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
const text = await page.locator('h1').textContent();
expect(text).toBe('Settings');
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot(); // clock + gravatar noise
```

**Why:** Locator assertions retry; soft groups failures; masked screenshots reduce flake.

## 12. Anti-flake

Retries apply in **CI only** (`retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`). Full test isolation (own data, own contexts). Use `page.clock` when flows depend on "now" (availability, calendars). Locale and timezone are pinned in config (`en-CA`, `America/Toronto`). Trace on first retry. Raising `retries` to 5 or forcing `workers: 1` is not a substitute for fixing root cause.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-06-15T10:00:00-04:00') });
await page.goto('/availability');
await expect(page.getByRole('cell', { name: '15' })).toBeVisible();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts — "make it pass"
retries: 5,
workers: 1, // everywhere, to paper over data collisions
await page.waitForTimeout(1000);
```

**Why:** Time, locale, and data isolation remove nondeterminism; retries/traces aid diagnosis, not denial.

## Refusals (hard stops)

Never weaken or delete an assertion to pass. Never heal a real product bug as a test fix. Never replace a role-based locator with CSS or XPath. Never use `waitForTimeout`, a hardcoded credential, or the `any` type. Each is a **conflict the agent raises**, not a preference it can be talked out of (enforced in `.cursor/hooks/` and `constitution.mdc`).

Examples use illustrative control names only — **do not** treat any label here as the real BuddyTime UI; confirm live copy in P7.
